{"about":"66df759e09c49faedd7f1b9e7ebf1d8ceeac2fed60fcd2cbf4ca3d2592289264","body":"The conjecture accepts the cost of being unable to quantify 'closeness to truth,' reducing scientific progress to a purely qualitative journey. This renders the preference for better-corroborated theories practically inert for decision-making in high-stakes environments (like engineering or medicine) where acting on a theory requires an assessment of risk and reliability. If we cannot distinguish between a theory that has survived one trivial test and one that has survived a thousand severe tests in terms of 'safety' or 'reliability,' the Popperian directive offers no rational basis for preferring the latter in action, contradicting the actual behavior of scientists the conjecture aims to justify.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"The cost of qualitative-only science"}